Absolutely. Let's start from zero and build the Indian Parliament topic for your SSC IMD General Awareness preparation.

🇮🇳 Indian Parliament — From Zero

First understand this one picture in your mind:

India has a Parliament at the Central/Union level.

Parliament has 3 parts:

President + Rajya Sabha + Lok Sabha = Parliament

This is the first thing to remember.

1. What is Parliament?

Parliament is the law-making body of the Union Government.

Think of it like this:

India
↓
Central/Union Government
↓
Parliament
↓
President + Rajya Sabha + Lok Sabha

The Parliament discusses and makes laws, approves government spending, discusses national issues, and holds the government accountable.

2. What is Lok Sabha?

Lok Sabha = House of the People

Think:

LOK = PEOPLE

So Lok Sabha represents the people of India.

Members of Lok Sabha are directly elected by the people.

For example, when people vote in a general election, they vote for candidates who will represent their constituencies in the Lok Sabha.

Lok Sabha important points
Point	Answer
Full name	House of the People
Members elected by	People directly
Term	Normally 5 years
Minimum age to become MP	25 years
Presiding officer	Speaker
House can be dissolved?	Yes
Money Bill	Lok Sabha has greater power
3. What is Rajya Sabha?

Rajya Sabha = Council of States

Think:

RAJYA = STATE

Rajya Sabha represents the States and Union Territories.

People generally do not directly vote for Rajya Sabha MPs.

Most Rajya Sabha members are elected by the elected members of State Legislative Assemblies.

Rajya Sabha important points
Point	Answer
Full name	Council of States
Represents	States & Union Territories
Members generally elected by	State Legislative Assembly members
Minimum age	30 years
Term of a member	6 years
House can be dissolved?	No
Presiding officer	Vice-President of India
Also called	Upper House
4. Lok Sabha vs Rajya Sabha

This is VERY IMPORTANT for SSC.

Feature	Lok Sabha	Rajya Sabha
Meaning	House of the People	Council of States
Represents	People	States & UTs
Election	Direct election	Mostly indirect election
Minimum age	25	30
Term	5 years normally	6 years per member
Permanent House?	❌ No	✅ Yes
Can be dissolved?	✅ Yes	❌ No
Presiding officer	Speaker	Vice-President
Money Bill	More power	Limited role
🧠 Super-easy memory

LOK → PEOPLE → DIRECT → 25 → 5 YEARS

RAJYA → STATES → INDIRECT → 30 → 6 YEARS

5. Who is an MP?

MP = Member of Parliament

A person sitting in either:

Lok Sabha, or
Rajya Sabha

is called an MP.

So:

Lok Sabha member = MP

Rajya Sabha member = MP

6. Who is the Speaker?

The Speaker presides over the Lok Sabha.

Think:

🗣️ Speaker → Lok Sabha

The Speaker conducts the proceedings of the Lok Sabha.

SSC question:

Who is the presiding officer of Lok Sabha?

✅ Speaker

7. Who is the Chairman of Rajya Sabha?

The Vice-President of India is the ex-officio Chairman of Rajya Sabha.

Think:

Vice-President → Rajya Sabha

So remember:

Lok Sabha → Speaker

Rajya Sabha → Vice-President/Chairman

8. What does "Upper House" and "Lower House" mean?

Don't get confused.

Lok Sabha

→ Lower House

Rajya Sabha

→ Upper House

This does NOT mean Rajya Sabha is more powerful.

It is simply the traditional terminology.

9. Who elects Lok Sabha members?

This is very important.

Suppose your area has a Lok Sabha constituency.

People vote:

People → Vote → Lok Sabha candidate → MP

Therefore:

✅ Lok Sabha = directly elected by people

10. Who elects Rajya Sabha members?

Here is the important difference.

Normally:

People → Elect State MLAs

Then:

Elected MLAs → Elect Rajya Sabha members

So it is generally an indirect election.

Remember:

Lok Sabha → People directly

Rajya Sabha → Elected MLAs generally

11. What is an MLA?

You will see this word frequently.

MLA = Member of Legislative Assembly

An MLA belongs to the State Legislature, not the Parliament.

So don't mix them.

Central level

Parliament

→ Lok Sabha
→ Rajya Sabha

Members = MP

State level

State Legislature

→ Legislative Assembly

Members = MLA

12. MP vs MLA
	MP	MLA
Full form	Member of Parliament	Member of Legislative Assembly
Works at	Central/Union level	State level
Parliament	Yes	No
Represents	Parliamentary constituency	Assembly constituency

Easy:

MP → Parliament

MLA → State Assembly

13. What is a Bill?

A Bill is basically a proposal for a new law or a change in an existing law.

Very simplified:

Bill → Parliament discusses → Passed by Parliament → President's assent → Law

For most ordinary bills, both Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha have roles.

14. What is a Money Bill?

A Money Bill deals with specific financial matters such as taxation and government expenditure.

Here comes an important SSC point:

Money Bill starts only in:

✅ Lok Sabha

Rajya Sabha can make recommendations, but it cannot reject or amend a Money Bill in the same way as Lok Sabha.

The Lok Sabha Speaker decides whether a bill is a Money Bill.

Memory:

💰 Money → Lok Sabha

15. Who is more powerful: Lok Sabha or Rajya Sabha?

Don't memorize "one is always more powerful."

Their powers are different.

But for Money Bills, Lok Sabha has a clearly stronger position.

Why?

Because Lok Sabha is directly elected by the people, and financial control is primarily exercised through the Lok Sabha.

16. What is the President doing in Parliament?

Remember our first formula:

Parliament = President + Rajya Sabha + Lok Sabha

So the President is also constitutionally a part of Parliament.

But the President is not an MP.

The President:

Summons Parliament
Can prorogue Parliament
Can dissolve Lok Sabha
Gives assent to bills
Addresses Parliament in certain situations
17. What is the Prime Minister's role?

The Prime Minister is the head of the Council of Ministers.

Think:

President
↓
Prime Minister
↓
Council of Ministers

The government is headed politically by the Prime Minister and Council of Ministers.

The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha.

That's an important SSC fact.

18. Why is Lok Sabha so important?

Because the Council of Ministers must have the confidence/support of the Lok Sabha.

If the government loses the confidence of the Lok Sabha, it may have to resign.

That's why you will frequently see:

Council of Ministers → collectively responsible to Lok Sabha

